   "item": {
    "subject_key": "biology",
    "unit_number": 1,
    "topic_code": "1.7",
    "topic_title": "Proteins",
    "stem": "A protein consists of one polypeptide chain. A version of this protein has one amino acid with a hydrophobic R group replaced by an amino acid with a charged R group. The chain length is unchanged, but the altered protein binds its usual partner less effectively. Which explanation best connects the amino acid replacement to reduced binding?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "The replacement leaves primary structure unchanged because chain length is unchanged, so reduced binding is unrelated to amino acid sequence.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Trap: confusing sequence with chain length. Primary structure is the amino acid sequence, so replacing one amino acid changes it even when the number of amino acids stays constant. Fix: Compare amino acid identities and order, not just chain length."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "The replacement breaks the peptide bonds responsible for tertiary folding, changing the protein's binding shape.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Trap: confusing backbone bonds with folding interactions. Peptide bonds connect amino acids in the chain; replacing an R group can change folding interactions without breaking those bonds. Fix: Distinguish bonds linking amino acids from interactions stabilizing the folded shape."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "The replacement changes R-group interactions, which can alter tertiary shape and the fit between the protein and its partner.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Changing the amino acid sequence changes primary structure. The new R group can interact differently with other R groups, altering folding and the three-dimensional shape needed for effective binding."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "The replacement changes quaternary structure by rearranging interactions among multiple polypeptide chains, changing the protein's binding shape.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Trap: confusing tertiary and quaternary structure. This protein has only one polypeptide chain, whereas quaternary structure involves interactions among multiple polypeptides. Fix: Count the polypeptide chains before assigning a structural level."
     }
    ]
   },
